---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import DownloadButton from "../components/DownloadButton.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Install — DST"
  description="Requirements and quick install for the Dune Server Tool."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Install"
    title="Quick install"
    intro="One Windows installer for an existing Self-Hosted server, a fresh local or LAN-hosted VM, or host-local Solo Mode."
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-12">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-dune-border bg-dune-surface p-8 mb-12">
      <ol class="space-y-5 text-dune-text">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span
            class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-dune-accent text-dune-bg font-semibold flex items-center justify-center text-sm"
            >1</span
          >
          <div>
            Download <span class="font-mono">DuneServerSetup.exe</span> from
            the latest release.
            <div class="mt-3">
              <DownloadButton />
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span
            class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-dune-accent text-dune-bg font-semibold flex items-center justify-center text-sm"
            >2</span
          >
          <div>
            Double-click. The installer walks you through it — one UAC
            prompt, then a Start Menu shortcut at
            <span class="font-mono text-dune-muted"
              >C:\Program Files\Dune Server\</span
            >.
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span
            class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-dune-accent text-dune-bg font-semibold flex items-center justify-center text-sm"
            >3</span
          >
          <div>
            Launch from <strong>Start Menu → Dune Server</strong>. The first
            launch opens the Setup Wizard, which asks whether you already have
            a Dune server, then walks you through a fresh install, a
            connection to an existing VM / SSH key, or
            <strong>Hyper-V over LAN</strong> to manage (and optionally
            remotely install) a VM on a separate Hyper-V host on your
            network. Answers are saved to
            <span class="font-mono text-dune-muted">%APPDATA%\DuneServer\</span
            >
            and survive reinstalls.
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">What you need</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-dune-muted leading-relaxed mb-12">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Windows 10 / 11</strong>. Solo Mode needs
        no VM. Self-Hosted mode needs Hyper-V locally, or access to a separate
        Hyper-V host over LAN.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">PowerShell 7</strong> (<span
          class="font-mono">pwsh</span
        >). The launcher prompts with a download link if missing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime</strong> —
        ships with Windows 11 and modern Windows 10. Falls back to your
        default browser if absent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Self-Hosted only:</strong> the
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Dune: Awakening Self-Hosted Server</strong>
        installed via Steam (provides battlegroup management and the VM image).
        Verified against the latest <strong
          class="text-dune-text">1.4.10.4</strong
        > build (July 31, 2026).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Self-Hosted only:</strong> the
        <strong class="text-dune-text">SSH private key</strong> for your VM —
        created automatically during Funcom's official self-hosted setup;
        usually at
        <span class="font-mono text-sm">%LOCALAPPDATA%\DuneAwakeningServer\sshKey</span
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">(Optional)</strong>
        <a
          href={`${base}remote`}
          class="underline decoration-dotted hover:text-dune-text"
          >Remote portal</a
        >
        — the responsive full Browser Portal on a phone, tablet, or PC, with
        optional Owner/Admin accounts. Recommended setup is a free Tailscale
        Funnel (no domain); a bring-your-own Cloudflare domain is supported as
        an advanced option.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Hyper-V over LAN <span class="text-dune-muted text-base font-normal">— optional, for a separate host</span></h2>
    <p class="text-dune-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Don't want the VM on this PC? The Setup Wizard's third option manages
      (and can remotely install) the Dune VM on a <strong class="text-dune-text">separate
      Hyper-V host</strong> on your local network — e.g. a headless server in
      another room. It's also reachable later from <strong class="text-dune-text">Settings
      → Hyper-V over LAN</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-dune-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Remote-host prerequisites</strong> —
        the host's <strong class="text-dune-text">PowerShell Remoting (WinRM)</strong>
        must be reachable from this PC. In a workgroup (no Active Directory
        domain), add the host to this PC's <span class="font-mono">TrustedHosts</span>
        first. The host also needs an <strong class="text-dune-text">external
        virtual switch</strong> for the VM's network adapter (the wizard shows
        the exact <span class="font-mono">New-VMSwitch</span> command to run
        on the host if none exists).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Existing VM or remote install</strong> —
        point DST at a host that already has a <span class="font-mono">dune-awakening</span>
        VM, or let DST provision one: it connects over PowerShell Remoting,
        downloads the server image on the host with SteamCMD (anonymous, no
        Steam login), and imports/starts the VM on a drive and switch you
        choose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Saved credential &amp; security</strong> —
        the host administrator credential (which doesn't need to match the
        Windows account DST runs as on this PC) is saved in
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Windows Credential Manager</strong>,
        scoped to your signed-in Windows user. It's never written to
        <span class="font-mono">dune-server.config</span>, logs, diagnostics,
        or any API response, and is reused automatically for every LAN call.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-dune-text">Local-mode fallback</strong> — turning
        the routing toggle off returns to managing a local VM on this PC and
        fully bypasses the LAN path; the saved credential is kept for next
        time until you explicitly remove it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Optional: run at Windows startup</h2>
    <p class="text-dune-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Off by default. Open <strong class="text-dune-text">Help → Run at
      Windows startup</strong> in the top toolbar to toggle it on. After
      that, DST launches automatically at every Windows sign-in — in the
      system tray with no app window — and closing the DuneShell window no
      longer stops the server. Toggle it back off the same way; takes
      effect at the next launch. The toggle is loopback-only — remote
      viewers can't enable it on your host. The uninstaller cleans up the
      scheduled task automatically.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Optional: keep serving while DST is closed</h2>
    <p class="text-dune-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Off by default. Open <strong class="text-dune-text">Help → Keep serving
      while DST is closed</strong> to install a background service so the
      Browser Portal, scheduled restarts, and Discord notifications keep
      running while the DST window is closed — including while your PC is
      locked — and load at sign-in. You must stay signed in to Windows; a full
      sign-out stops remote access. Host-only (remote viewers can't enable it),
      and the uninstaller removes the service.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Where things live</h2>
    <div
      class="rounded-lg border border-dune-border bg-dune-surface overflow-hidden text-sm"
    >
      <dl class="divide-y divide-dune-border">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-[1fr_2fr] gap-4 px-5 py-3">
          <dt class="text-dune-muted">Install dir</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-dune-text">C:\Program Files\Dune Server\</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-[1fr_2fr] gap-4 px-5 py-3">
          <dt class="text-dune-muted">Config &amp; state</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-dune-text">%APPDATA%\DuneServer\</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-[1fr_2fr] gap-4 px-5 py-3">
          <dt class="text-dune-muted">Local portal</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-dune-text">
            http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/?t=&lt;token&gt;
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-xl border border-dune-border bg-dune-surface p-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Remote access</h2>
      <p class="text-dune-muted leading-relaxed">
        Remote access is optional. v14 uses the same responsive Browser Portal
        as the desktop app, with host-created Owner/Admin accounts and
        role-enforced navigation and APIs. The recommended path is a Tailscale
        Funnel; a Cloudflare custom domain remains available for advanced
        setups. Use the dedicated guide for account setup, role boundaries,
        transport security, and revocation instead of exposing DST's local port
        directly.
      </p>
      <a
        href={`${base}remote`}
        class="inline-flex mt-4 text-dune-accent hover:text-dune-accent-soft"
      >
        Open the remote access guide →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
